# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/001_usual_arithmetic_conversions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 001_usual_arithmetic_conversions
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Usual arithmetic conversions across mixed signedness and rank, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every operand is within range and every conversion is well defined. The unsigned product
is computed modulo 2^32, which is defined behaviour; no signed type ever overflows. The
signed operand mixed with an unsigned operand is masked with 0x7fffffff so it is provably
non-negative, which also keeps the strict warning gate clean without any deviation. No
shift appears. No aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, no object modified twice
between sequence points, at most one side-effecting argument per call.
The _Generic controlling expressions are unevaluated, so they assert conversion result
types without performing any conversion at run time. That is what lets the int operand of
the int-with-unsigned-int probe be negative while no negative value is ever converted to
an unsigned type at run time.
Each argument that reads volatile storage reads it more than once in only one place, the
unsigned product, where the operands are multiplied; the value of a product does not
depend on which operand is read first, so nothing here depends on unspecified evaluation
order.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Plain char is never used as an arithmetic type and no object of plain char type is
declared, because plain char signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but
unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64. The type appears only as the const char * of the
returned type names and of printf's format parameter, where no character value is read,
printed or converted, so that measured difference cannot reach this program.
long and unsigned long are never used, because their width was measured as 4 bytes on i686
and 8 bytes on the other three targets; only int, unsigned int, unsigned short, unsigned
char, long long and unsigned long long appear as arithmetic types, and every one of them
has the same width on all four supported targets. No pointer value is printed.
long long represents every value of unsigned int on all four supported targets, so the
usual arithmetic conversions select long long rather than unsigned long long for the
long-long-wins case identically on every backend.
Nothing is narrowed as a result: this record restricts no target, disables no oracle and
takes no warning-gate deviation, because each implementation-defined property above is
removed by construction rather than excused, which keeps all four targets and all three
oracles in force for this program.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_uint_common=4294967294
fold_ushort_promotes=-2
fold_uchar_promotes=-2
fold_llong_wins=0
fold_ullong_wins=5
run_uint_common=4294967294
run_ushort_promotes=-2
run_uchar_promotes=-2
run_llong_wins=0
run_ullong_wins=5
type_int_uint=unsigned int
type_ushort_int=int
type_uint_llong=long long
type_uint_ullong=unsigned long long
END
